course: everyday
lesson: 216
title: Privacy, screens, sleep and fairness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your body and toilet time are private: you close the bathroom door, and people knock before coming in.
explain: Too much screen time can make you grumpy and sleep badly, because bright screens and exciting games keep your brain awake; breaks, time outside and agreed limits help.
explain: Every family decides when a child is ready for a phone, and many children your age do not have one yet.
explain: Sharing kindly is good, but your sweets are yours, so sharing is your choice; playing fairly matters too, because cheating spoils the game.
explain: A boy in Ottawa cannot sleep after playing on a tablet in bed; when he puts it away an hour before bed and reads a book, he falls asleep faster.
fact: Using the toilet is private, so you close the door and others knock first.
fact: Too much screen time can affect your mood and your sleep.
fact: Taking breaks, going outside and keeping to agreed limits help with screen time.
fact: Many children aged 9 or 10 do not have their own phone.
fact: Each family decides when it is right for a child to have a phone.
fact: Screens before bed keep your brain awake.
fact: Reading or listening to calm sounds before bed helps you fall asleep.
fact: Sharing your own sweets is kind, but it is your choice.
fact: Cheating spoils a game, and people stop wanting to play with a cheat.
fact: A good compliment is specific and true.
fact: Every body grows and changes at its own speed.
exercise: You are using the toilet at home. What is normal? | You close the door and others knock first | You leave the door open in case someone needs you | Anyone can walk in, as it is your family | You lock everyone out of the house
exercise: Why do people close the bathroom door? | Bodies and toilet time are private | The bathroom gets cold otherwise in winter | It is a rule only for the grown-ups | To keep the smell of the soap inside
exercise: After three hours of games you feel grumpy and snappy. What helps? | Take a break, go outside and keep to agreed limits | Play another hour to cheer yourself up before tea | Switch to a different game instead | Eat sweets in front of the screen
exercise: Why can lots of screen time make you grumpy? | It can affect your mood and your sleep | Screens slowly make your eyes change colour | Games use up all your energy food | It makes your brain grow too fast
exercise: Everyone in your group seems to talk about their phones and you have none. What is true? | Many children your age do not have a phone yet | You are the only child in the world without one | Your parents must buy one right away | Children without phones cannot have friends
exercise: Who decides when a child is ready for a phone? | The child's own family | The school head teacher | The shop that sells the phone | The child's best friend
exercise: You cannot fall asleep. What is a good thing to try? | Put screens away and read or listen to calm sounds | Watch videos in bed until you finally start to feel tired | Play a fast game to wear yourself out | Check messages under the covers
exercise: Why are screens bad just before bed? | They keep your brain awake | They make the room too cold | They use up all your dreams | They make your bed uncomfortable
exercise: Your little brother wants half of your sweets. What is true? | Sharing kindly is good, but it is your choice | You must always give him exactly half, it's the rule | You must never share your own things | He can take them if you say no
exercise: You could win a board game by secretly moving your piece. What do you do? | Play fairly | Move it when nobody is looking | Cheat only if you are losing badly | Cheat, since it is only a game
exercise: Why does cheating spoil a game? | It is not fair and people won't want to play | It makes the game finish much too quickly to enjoy | Board games break when you cheat | Cheating is only wrong in sport
exercise: Which is the best compliment for a friend's painting? | "I love how you mixed the blue in the sky" | "It's nice, I suppose" | "It's better than everyone else's, really" | "Your paintings are always amazing"
exercise: Someone teases you for being the shortest in the class. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own speed | Short children stay short for ever | Something must be wrong with your body | You should eat much more to catch up
exercise: Your cousin is taller than you though you are the same age. Why? | Bodies grow and change at different times | Your cousin eats more carrots than you, so grows faster | You did not sleep enough last year | Your cousin is older inside
test: A girl in Texas walks into the bathroom while her brother is in it. What should she have done? | Knocked and waited for an answer | Walked straight in, since he is only family | Shouted his name and gone in | Opened the door slowly
test: Ollie is cross and tired every evening after gaming all afternoon. What would most likely help? | Shorter screen time with breaks outside | A bigger screen, so his eyes don't get so tired | Gaming later into the night | Gaming with the sound off
test: Which is true about children and phones? | Each family chooses the right time for its children | Every 9-year-old has a phone | A child without a phone always falls behind at school | Phones are needed to make friends
test: Lily lies awake after watching videos in bed. What change should help most? | No screens before bed, and a book instead | A brighter screen in bed | Watching scarier videos | A video of someone sleeping
test: You have a bag of sweets and your friend has none. Which is right? | You may share, and it is your choice | You must give her every single one of them | Keeping them is stealing from her | She has the right to half
test: In a game of cards, Ben keeps peeking at other players' hands. What will most likely happen? | The others will not want to play with him | He will become the most popular player at the table | The game will become more fun | Nobody will mind as he wins
test: Which compliment will make a classmate feel best? | "Your speech made me laugh at the part about your dog" | "Good job" said without looking | "You're nearly as good as me" | "Not bad for you"
test: Ella grew a lot this year and her best friend hardly at all. What is true? | Both are normal, as bodies grow at different times | Ella must be ill, because she grew so fast in one year | Her friend will never grow | They must have eaten different breakfasts
test: my lil bro just barges in when im on the loo | The toilet is private; he should knock first | That's normal, doors stay open | You should leave the door open | Lock everyone out of the house
test: everyone in my class has a phone, well, nearly, can i get one | Many 9 and 10 year olds don't; each family decides | Every child must have one | You'll fall behind without one | Buy one in secret
test: Lily says nice drawing, is that a good compliment? | A good compliment is specific and true | Yes, short ones are always the best ones | Yes, any compliment is good | No, compliments are rude
test: have to share my sweets with my brother?? | It's kind to share, but it's your choice | Yes, it's the law | No, never share sweets | Only if he asks three times
explain: A girl in Florida posted a photo in her school uniform with her street in the background; a stranger worked out where she lived and began sending her messages, and her parents had to call the police.
explain: A boy in Scotland played games on his phone in bed every night; he became so tired that he fell asleep in class and his grades dropped.
fact: Photos and posts can show your school, street or home, and strangers can use these clues to find you.
fact: Screens in bed keep children awake later, and too little sleep makes learning and moods worse.
test: posting a pic in my school jumper, front of my house lol | Don't, strangers could find your school and home | Fine if it's a nice pic | Fine if you crop your face | Only post it on weekends
test: a person i met in a game wants my address to send a present | Don't give it, tell a parent | Give it, presents are always nice | Give your school address instead | Ask them to post it to a friend
test: Why did the boy in Scotland fall asleep in class? | Using his phone in bed kept him awake too late | Phones make you hungry | Phones are heavy to carry | Screens make eyes grow
test: did u hear a kid posted a pic in his school jumper outside his house and some creepy guy turned up?? what's the real lesson? | Photos can show your school or home to strangers | Only post photos in your pyjamas | Strangers can't see photos you post | It only happens to famous kids
test: my mate says everyone in year 5 has a phone except me, literally everyone. what's probably exaggerated? | Everyone; many 9–10 year olds have no phone | That some children in year 5 have their own phones | That phones are popular | That families decide about phones
test: apparently that boy cheated at Monopoly at the sleepover and now nobody's playing with him ever. pass it round? | No, gossip doesn't help; cheating spoils games though | Yes, so nobody plays with him | Yes, and add that he cheats at school and in tests too | Yes, if it's true it's fine to spread
exam: Identify the effect of the use of screens in bed upon children. | Children remain awake later and learning and mood suffer | Children fall asleep sooner and more deeply | Screens in bed improve the memory | Screens in bed have no effect on sleep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the age at which a child receives a phone? | Each family determines when it is appropriate | All children receive one at the age of nine years exactly | The law requires a phone by the age of ten | Schools decide the age for each child
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning photographs posted online. | They may disclose one's school, street or home to strangers | They are visible only to one's friends | They cannot be copied or shared | They reveal nothing of one's location
exam: Ascertain the characteristics of a good compliment. | It is specific and true | It is vague and exaggerated | It concerns only appearance | It is given in order to obtain favours
